          "source_ids": [
            "sections.0",
            "sections.1"
          ],
          "text": "You were in hospital after a road traffic collision.\n\nThe team found:\n- A broken left shin bone (closed left tibial shaft fracture)\n- A small tear in your spleen (grade II splenic laceration)\n- Broken ribs on the left side (ribs 7–9) with a small collection of blood near your lung (haemothorax)\n- Small fractures of a bone in your lower back (L1 transverse process fractures)"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.1",
          "heading": "what_we_did",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.2",
            "sections.3"
          ],
          "text": "The team ran a number of tests, including CT scans, X-rays and blood tests. Your blood levels were stable by Day 2 and again on Day 4. The CT scan of your spleen on Day 4 showed it was improving. Your chest X-ray on Day 4 was satisfactory.\n\nThe team treated you across several specialties.\n\n- On Day 2, surgeons fixed your broken shin bone with a metal rod (intramedullary nailing).\n- Your spleen injury was treated without an operation. The team monitored you closely with regular checks and blood tests.\n- A drain was put into your chest to remove the blood near your lung. It was taken out on Day 4.\n- A physiotherapist helped you to move around. You are partially weight-bearing on your left leg, as advised by the orthopaedic team.\n- You were given an injection (enoxaparin) to help prevent blood clots while you were in hospital."
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.2",
          "heading": "medicines",
          "source_ids": [],
          "text": "{{MEDICATIONS}}"
        },
        {
          "section_id": "c_sections.3",
          "heading": "next_steps",
          "source_ids": [
            "sections.7"
          ],
          "text": "- You have a follow-up appointment in the orthopaedic fracture clinic at 2 weeks.\n- The general surgery team will do a telephone review about your spleen at 6 weeks.\n- The respiratory and thoracics team do not need to see you routinely after discharge."
        },
